 #edb/ref 17592186492559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492818 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492818 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492818 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492818 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe0672c6-a8a1-54ee-b67e-0f22d1bf3419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492824 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto the whole of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0592700d-3dcd-547f-b289-799124f15c20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492824 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{1}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=0.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{2}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=1.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Indeed, ${T}_{3}$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492827 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492827 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492827 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492827 #edb/ref 1071 "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ea7759f-f796-5f54-9f1a-8b557e8042a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492827 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(1,3),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-0}{1-0}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=3x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492832 #edb/ref 1071 "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18ff2792-817e-5c06-978e-de39e8bf5d7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492832 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,2)$ and $(4,8),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8-2}{4-1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492866 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492866 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492866 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492866 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n\nII. $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63cc3457-67ea-5db0-a46f-35ae7d0b8b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492866 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace $\\{0\\},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The image of $T$ contains the zero vector $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
